course: dictionary
lesson: 298
title: Spotting an anachronism
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An anachronism is something that is in the wrong time. It belongs to another period, so it could not have been there in the story or picture. «A Roman soldier with a wristwatch» is an anachronism, because wristwatches did not exist in Roman times.
explain: To spot one, ask: could this thing have existed at this time? If the thing is younger than the scene, you have found an anachronism. «A Viking with a smartphone» is a clear example, because smartphones came along very much later than the Vikings.
explain: An anachronism can also be a person, custom or thing that seems old-fashioned and belongs to an earlier time. «A glaring anachronism» is an obvious mistake, and «a bit of an anachronism» means something a little out of date. Spot the anachronism is a fun game in history lessons.
explain: Words can be anachronisms too. «OK» was first printed in America in 1839, so a knight saying «OK» in a film is out of time. Thinking carefully about dates helps you catch mistakes that writers and film-makers make, or sometimes make on purpose as a joke.
fact: An anachronism is something that belongs to a different time from the one it appears in.
fact: «Anachronism» is a noun, and its plural is anachronisms.
fact: «A wristwatch on a Roman soldier» is an anachronism, because wristwatches came much later than the Romans.
fact: An anachronism tells the reader that a writer or film-maker made a mistake, or is playing a joke.
fact: Old-fashioned people, customs or things can be anachronisms when they seem to belong to an earlier time.
exercise: «A Roman soldier checks the time on his wristwatch.» What is the anachronism? | *The wristwatch | The Roman times | The soldier | The checking
exercise: Which of these would be an anachronism in a film about knights? | A knight wearing armour | A castle with stone walls | A knight riding a horse | *A knight saying «OK»
exercise: «A glaring anachronism» is a phrase. What does glaring suggest here? | *An obvious mistake | A shiny piece of armour | A very bright light | A famous painting
test: Why is a car in a film about the Romans an anachronism? | *Cars did not exist in Roman times | Romans liked cars very much | Cars are always in films | Cars are very old
test: A film shows a Viking holding a smartphone. What has the film-maker done? | Made a spelling mistake | Shown a real Viking village | *Made an anachronism | Used a synonym
exam: Why is «the knight pulled out his wristwatch» an anachronism? | Knights were always late for battles | Wristwatches are too heavy for knights | Knights always carried watches | *Wristwatches were made long after knights, so they are in the wrong time
